# Modern Dart & Flutter Practices

## Dart 3 Features

Use pattern matching, records, and sealed classes instead of manual type checks or verbose data holders:

```dart
// ❌ BAD
if (result is Success) {
  final value = (result as Success).value;
}

// ✅ GOOD
switch (result) {
  case Success(:final value) => doSomething(value),
  case Failure(:final error) => handleError(error),
}
```

```dart
// ❌ BAD — one-off class just to return two values
class MinMax { final int min, max; const MinMax(this.min, this.max); }
MinMax findRange(List<int> values) => MinMax(values.reduce(min), values.reduce(max));

// ✅ GOOD — record for a small, anonymous bundle or tuple return value
(int min, int max) findRange(List<int> values) =>
    (values.reduce(min), values.reduce(max));
```

> **Records vs. classes**: records are ideal for lightweight, anonymous data bundles — return values, local tuples, and grouping unrelated values. For domain models with behaviour, validation, or long lifetimes (e.g. `User`, `Order`, `Product`), continue using named classes or freezed data classes.

## Null Safety

Prefer non-nullable types. Avoid `!` (bang operator) — use `??`, `?.`, `if-null`, or early returns instead:

```dart
// ❌ BAD
final name = user!.name!;

// ✅ GOOD
final name = user?.name ?? 'Unknown';
```

## const

Use `const` constructors and literals wherever possible to aid tree-shaking and performance:

```dart
// ❌ BAD
Text(AppStrings.greeting, style: TextStyle(fontSize: 16))

// ✅ GOOD
const Text(AppStrings.greeting, style: TextStyle(fontSize: 16))
```

## async / await

Always use `async`/`await` over raw `.then()`/`.catchError()` callbacks:

```dart
// ❌ BAD
fetchUser().then((u) => setState(() => user = u)).catchError((e) => print(e));

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  final u = await fetchUser();
  // handle result
} catch (e) {
  // handle error
}
```

## State Management (Riverpod)

Use providers for all shared or async state. Never use `setState` for anything beyond purely local ephemeral UI state:

```dart
// ❌ BAD — global mutable state or setState for business logic
setState(() => _user = await fetchUser());

// ✅ GOOD
final userProvider = FutureProvider<User>((ref) => fetchUser());
// consume with ref.watch(userProvider) in widgets
```

## Modern Collection Syntax

Prefer collection `if`, `for`, and spread operators over imperative `add`/`addAll` calls:

```dart
// ❌ BAD
final items = <Widget>[];
items.add(const Header());
if (isAdmin) items.add(const AdminPanel());

// ✅ GOOD
final items = [
  const Header(),
  if (isAdmin) const AdminPanel(),
];
```
